Generating an embedding for a piece of text, using a dedicated
embedding model (via an API call, conceptually similar to lesson 1's
llmClient.generate, but producing a vector instead of text):

const queryEmbedding = await embeddingClient.embed('affordable laptop');
// queryEmbedding is now an array of numbers, e.g.
// [0.0234, -0.0891, 0.1502, ..., 0.0067] (often 768 or 1536 numbers)

const docEmbedding = await embeddingClient.embed('budget-friendly notebook computer');

Measuring genuine semantic similarity between two embeddings using
COSINE SIMILARITY - the standard, mathematical measure of how
closely two vectors point in the same direction:

function cosineSimilarity(vecA: number[], vecB: number[]): number {
  const dotProduct = vecA.reduce((sum, a, i) => sum + a * vecB[i], 0);
  const magnitudeA = Math.sqrt(vecA.reduce((sum, a) => sum + a * a, 0));
  const magnitudeB = Math.sqrt(vecB.reduce((sum, b) => sum + b * b, 0));
  return dotProduct / (magnitudeA * magnitudeB);
  // returns a value from -1 (genuinely opposite meaning) to 1
  // (genuinely identical meaning), with values close to 1 indicating
  // genuinely strong semantic similarity
}

const similarity = cosineSimilarity(queryEmbedding, docEmbedding);
// e.g., 0.89 - a genuinely HIGH similarity score, correctly
// reflecting these two phrases' genuinely similar meaning, despite
// sharing almost no actual words in common

