sections.content:
EXACT (brute-force) search, lesson 2's approach: GUARANTEED to find
the genuinely closest vectors, but its cost grows LINEARLY with the
number of documents - genuinely impractical past a few thousand vectors.

APPROXIMATE NEAREST NEIGHBOR (ANN) search, what vector databases
actually implement: uses a specialized index structure (commonly
HNSW - Hierarchical Navigable Small World graphs) that organizes
vectors so a search can skip comparing against the vast majority of
them, finding vectors that are GENUINELY VERY LIKELY, but not always
mathematically GUARANTEED, to be the true closest matches:

const vectorDb = new VectorDatabase({ indexType: 'hnsw' });

await vectorDb.upsert({
  id: 'doc-1',
  vector: documentEmbedding,
  metadata: { title: 'Budget-friendly notebook computer' }
});

const results = await vectorDb.query({
  vector: queryEmbedding,
  topK: 5
  // returns the 5 vectors the HNSW index believes are closest -
  // genuinely, very likely correct, but not mathematically
  // GUARANTEED to be the exact, true 5 closest vectors in every case
});

