// AT SMALL SCALE (lesson 2's exact scenario - 3 documents):
// Brute-force: 3 cosine-similarity computations, genuinely instant
// ANN/vector database: would still work, but its added indexing
// overhead provides ZERO genuine benefit at this tiny scale -
// exactly Module 27 lesson 5's 'caching the wrong layer provides
// zero benefit' argument, now applied to vector search specifically

// AT REAL, PRODUCTION SCALE (1,000,000 documents):
// Brute-force: 1,000,000 cosine-similarity computations PER QUERY -
// genuinely, measurably too slow for real-time search (potentially
// multiple seconds, crossing Module 24's own UX-latency thresholds)

// ANN via a vector database's HNSW index: genuinely, measurably
// fast (often single-digit milliseconds), since the index structure
// lets the search skip comparing against the vast majority of the
// 1,000,000 vectors entirely, examining only a small, genuinely
// promising subset guided by the index's own pre-built navigation structure

// THE EVIDENCE-BASED DECISION (directly extending lesson 2's own
// scale-appropriate tool selection): brute force remains genuinely
// correct and sufficient at lesson 2's small scale; a dedicated
// vector database with ANN indexing becomes genuinely NECESSARY
// specifically once the document count crosses into territory where
// brute force's linear cost genuinely, measurably degrades real-time
// search latency - exactly the SAME evidence-based threshold
// discipline Module 19 lesson 5 applied to deciding when an index is
// actually justified for a traditional database query

